          {
            id: "alternative-choice",
            title: "The Alternative Choice Close",
            description: "Replace yes/no questions with choice-based closes that assume the sale.",
            duration: "5 min",
            content: "## The Alternative Choice Close\n\nInstead of asking if they are ready to buy, offer two options: Does 60-month or 72-month financing work better? Assume the purchase and focus on logistics.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** The Alternative Choice shifts thinking from ____ to ____.\nA) price to value\nB) should I buy to how should I buy\nC) now to later\nD) car to truck\n*Answer: B \u2014 The Alternative Choice Close reframes the decision from a yes/no question to a preference between two positive outcomes.*\n\n**Q2:** When to deploy?\nA) Immediately on greeting\nB) After value is established\nC) Only after they say yes\nD) Never\n*Answer: B \u2014 Deploying after value is established ensures the customer understands what they are choosing between before making a selection.*\n\n**Q3:** Best Alternative Choice question?\nA) Are you buying this car?\nB) Would you prefer the red or blue?\nC) What color?\nD) Should I call my manager?\n*Answer: B \u2014 Offering two desirable options assumes the purchase and lets the customer focus on how to buy rather than whether to buy.*\n\n**Q4:** Why avoid offering one bad option?\nA) It is illegal\nB) Customers see through manipulation\nC) Takes too long\nD) Both are equally good\n*Answer: B \u2014 Customers quickly detect when one option is intentionally unappealing, which erodes trust and damages credibility.*\n\n**Q5:** What follows after asking the choice question?\nA) Answer objections immediately\nB) Pause and let them process\nC) Repeat the question\nD) Move to paperwork\n*Answer: B \u2014 Pausing after the choice question gives the customer space to mentally commit to one of the options without pressure.*\n\n**Q6:** Which is NOT effective?\nA) 60-month or 72-month?\nB) Friday or Saturday delivery?\nC) Are you SURE you do not want it?\nD) Red exterior or blue?\n*Answer: C \u2014 A sarcastic or guilt-inducing question creates pressure and damages rapport rather than guiding a collaborative decision.*\n\n**Q7:** This close works best with:\nA) High pressure\nB) A thorough needs assessment confirming fit\nC) Multiple calls\nD) Immediate discount\n*Answer: B \u2014 The Alternative Choice Close is most effective when the customer already sees clear value from a thorough needs assessment.*"
          },
          {
            id: "assumptive-trial",
            title: "The Assumptive Trial Close",
            description: "Test commitment right after the test drive before final negotiations.",
            duration: "5 min",
            content: "## The Assumptive Trial Close\n\nRight after the test drive, ask: If we can get the payments where you need them, is this the exact car you want to drive home today? This establishes mental ownership before numbers.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** Ideal timing for Assumptive Trial Close?\nA) During greeting\nB) Right after the test drive\nC) After they leave\nD) During paperwork\n*Answer: B \u2014 Right after the test drive is when excitement peaks, making it the ideal moment to test commitment before discussing numbers.*\n\n**Q2:** What does this close achieve?\nA) Forces immediate signature\nB) Isolates price as the only remaining variable\nC) Replaces the test drive\nD) Guarantees sale\n*Answer: B \u2014 The Assumptive Trial Close narrows the conversation to price as the only remaining variable, streamlining the path to close.*\n\n**Q3:** Which phrase plants mental ownership?\nA) If we can get the payments\nB) Is this the exact car\nC) Drive home today\nD) Where you need them\n*Answer: C \u2014 Drive home today creates a vivid mental image of ownership, which is more powerful than abstract phrases about payments or fit.*\n\n**Q4:** If they say not sure, respond with:\nA) Offer a discount\nB) End interaction\nC) What would need to be different?\nD) Repeat the question\n*Answer: C \u2014 Asking what would need to be different invites the customer to reveal the real objection rather than shutting down the conversation.*\n\n**Q5:** This close is best described as:\nA) High-pressure tactic\nB) Temperature check before negotiations\nC) Confusion tactic\nD) Replacement for sales process\n*Answer: B \u2014 This close functions as a temperature check that reveals remaining objections before entering formal negotiations.*\n\n**Q6:** Only use with buyers who already agreed?\nA) True\nB) False\n*Answer: B \u2014 The Assumptive Trial Close works with any buyer who has completed the test drive, regardless of whether they have made prior commitments.*\n\n**Q7:** After successful trial close, next step:\nA) More trial closes\nB) Send them home\nC) Confidently move to numbers\nD) Start over\n*Answer: C \u2014 Once the customer confirms the vehicle is right, confidently moving to numbers maintains momentum and signals professionalism.*"
          },
          {
            id: "either-or-objections",
            title: "Either/Or Objection Handling",
            description: "Isolate the real objection by framing it as an either/or choice.",
            duration: "5 min",
            content: "## Either/Or Objection Handling\n\nWhen customer says I need to think about it, ask: Is it the trade-in value or the monthly payment? Isolate the real issue and provide a concrete solution.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** Primary purpose of Either/Or?\nA) Pressure to buy\nB) Transform vague objection into solvable problem\nC) Confuse customer\nD) End conversation\n*Answer: B \u2014 Either/Or Objection Handling converts vague stall objections into specific, solvable problems you can address directly.*\n\n**Q2:** Best Either/Or for I need to think?\nA) Why, what is wrong?\nB) Is it the trade-in or the payment?\nC) OK, call me later\nD) We have other cars\n*Answer: B \u2014 Trade-in value and payment are the two most common hidden concerns behind the I need to think about it objection.*\n\n**Q3:** What tone to use?\nA) Aggressive\nB) Curious and helpful\nC) Sarcastic\nD) Indifferent\n*Answer: B \u2014 A curious and helpful tone makes the customer feel you are solving a problem together rather than pressuring them.*\n\n**Q4:** Why say I need to think?\nA) Wasting time\nB) Unspoken concern\nC) Already buying elsewhere\nD) Do not like you\n*Answer: B \u2014 I need to think is almost always a cover for an unspoken concern the customer is not ready to articulate directly.*\n\n**Q5:** After identifying real issue:\nA) Celebrate\nB) Provide concrete solution\nC) Ask another Either/Or\nD) Walk away\n*Answer: B \u2014 Once the true objection is identified, the essential next step is offering a concrete and actionable solution.*\n\n**Q6:** NOT a good Either/Or?\nA) Trade-in or payment?\nB) Color or features?\nC) Why do not you just buy it?\nD) Timing or financing?\n*Answer: C \u2014 A confrontational question like Why don't you just buy it creates defensiveness rather than uncovering the real objection.*\n\n**Q7:** Best combined with:\nA) Active listening and curiosity\nB) Aggressive negotiation\nC) Ignoring body language\nD) Multiple calls over weeks\n*Answer: A \u2014 Active listening and genuine curiosity make the Either/Or technique feel collaborative rather than interrogating.*"
          },
          {
            id: "micro-agreements",
            title: "Micro-Agreements",
            description: "Build toward the close with layers of small agreements throughout the sale.",
            duration: "5 min",
            content: "## Micro-Agreements\n\nInstead of one big close, build commitment incrementally. Confirm small agreements: Does this cargo space solve your issue? By paperwork time, they have agreed multiple times.\n\n### Quick Quiz\n\n**Q1:** Core principle of Micro-Agreements?\nA) Repeat same question\nB) Build through layers of small incremental yeses\nC) Use legal jargon\nD) Small discounts throughout\n*Answer: B \u2014 Micro-Agreements build commitment incrementally through a series of small, easy-to-agree-to confirmations throughout the sale.*\n\n**Q2:** What makes it psychologically effective?\nA) People enjoy pressure\nB) People want consistency with prior commitments\nC) People do not notice small questions\nD) People buy on impulse\n*Answer: B \u2014 People have a strong psychological need to remain consistent with commitments they have already made, so each small yes builds toward the final yes.*\n\n**Q3:** Good micro-agreement during walkaround?\nA) You are buying this, right?\nB) Does this cargo config solve your space issue?\nC) This car is the best, is not it?\nD) Sign here now\n*Answer: B \u2014 Asking whether a specific feature solves their problem invites confirmation without pressure and reinforces the vehicle value.*\n\n**Q4:** When NOT to use a micro-agreement?\nA) During needs assessment\nB) During test drive\nC) When customer just raised a genuine concern\nD) During feature demo\n*Answer: C \u2014 Using a micro-agreement right after a customer raises a concern feels dismissive and breaks rapport instead of building it.*\n\n**Q5:** How many micro-agreements per sale?\nA) One\nB) 4-6, spaced naturally\nC) At least 20\nD) None, just close at end\n*Answer: B \u2014 4 to 6 micro-agreements spaced naturally throughout the sale provide enough consistency-building without feeling scripted or repetitive.*\n\n**Q6:** Best post-test-drive question?\nA) On a scale of 1-10, how well does this fit?\nB) You are buying, right?\nC) Did you like it?\nD) Are we done?\n*Answer: A \u2014 A scale question gathers actionable feedback while keeping the conversation open and low-pressure.*\n\n**Q7:** Micro-agreements eliminate need for final close?\nA) True\nB) False\n*Answer: B \u2014 Micro-agreements build valuable momentum but do not replace the need for a clear, confident final close to seal the deal.*"
          }
